===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|divination}} [[divination|Divination]] by the [[interpretation]] of [[dream#Noun|dreams]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=T. Fahd|chapter=KIHĀNA|editors=[[w:Clifford Edmund Bosworth|C[lifford] E[dmund] Bosworth]]; E[meri J.] van Donzel; [[w:Bernard Lewis|B[ernard] Lewis]]; [[w:Charles Pellat|Ch[arles] Pellat]]|title=[[w:Encyclopaedia of Islam|The Encyclopaedia of Islam]]|edition=new|location=Leiden|publisher=[[w:Brill Publishers|E. J. Brill]]|year=1979|volume=V|section=fascicules 79–80 (ḴH̱EMS̱H̱IL–ḴIRĀ’A)|page=101|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cJQ3AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA101|column=1|oclc=917237494|passage=The absence of '''oneiromancy''' among the divinatory techniques is justified by the fact that it occupies in Islam a higher place than that of divination in the sphere of religious inspiration.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Abraham Bos|A[braham] P. Bos]]|chapter=''Manteia'' in Aristotle|title=Cosmic and Meta-cosmic Theology in Aristotle’s Lost Dialogues|series=Brill’s Studies in Intellectual History|seriesvolume=16|year=1989|page=164|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=t3AGz3wyjwgC&pg=PA164|isbn=978-90-04-09155-9|passage=7. ''Did {{w|Aristotle}}'s attitude to '''oneiromancy''' change?'' / In the foregoing sections we sketched the consistent and lucid oneirology which various indirect sources attribute to Aristotle.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jean-Marie Husser|tlr=Jill M. Munro|chapter=|title=Dreams and Dream Narratives in the Biblical World|series=The Biblical Seminar|seriesvolume=63|location=Sheffield, South Yorkshire|publisher=w:Sheffield Academic Press|year=1999|page=30|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=7vpMI0pi_KQC&pg=PA30|isbn=978-1-85075-968-3|passage=2. Deductive '''Oneiromancy''' / This form of '''oneiromancy''' aims to decipher the messages contained in ordinary, everyday dreams, messages thought to presage future events in the life of the dreamer.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|in a weak sense}} The interpretation of dreams.
#: {{synonyms|en|oneirocriticism|oneirology|oneiroscopy}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Samuel Jackson|chapter=Of Sleep and Dreams|title=The Principles of Medicine, Founded on the Structure and Functions of the Animal Organism|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=[[w:Henry Charles Carey|Carey]] & [[w:Isaac Lea|Lea]]|year=1832|section=§&nbsp;2 (Of Dreams)|page=306|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gtI0AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA306|oclc=2698716|passage=The general tenor of ideas in dreaming is the same as that with which the mind is occupied awake; and thus may be revealed, in dreams, the state of the mind, the secret hopes and wishes, the profoundly hidden thought, and often the circumstances of the dreamer. The knowledge of this fact, and its dextrous application, by an acute observer, with the credulous, compose the art of '''oneiromancy'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|divination}} An [[act#Noun|act]] of such divination or dream-interpretation.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=John Ashburner|title=IV. ''On the Silent Influence of the Will.'' By Dr. Ashburner. Communicated in a Letter to Dr. [[w:John Elliotson|[John] Elliotson]].|journal=[[w:The Zoist|The Zoist: A Journal of Cerebral Physiology & Mesmerism, and Their Applications to Human Welfare]]|location=London|publisher=Hippolyte Ballière, publisher,{{nb...|219, Regent Street}}; Paris: J. B. Ballière,{{nb...|rue de l'Ecole du Medecine}}; Leipzig: T. O. Weigel|month=October|year=1847|year_published=1848|volume=V|issue=XIX|page=265|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hnE7AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA265|oclc=187499064|passage=Finding that I could produce obedience to my silent will in somnambulists to the extent of inducing some to lift the hand to either cheek— {{...}} I resolved to try if I could influence some of my patients to obey me in the performance of a train of actions. This was education, and this I take it requires the high order of patience to bring to the degree of perfection which we have seen exhibited in the '''oneiromancie''' of the cook-maid Mlle. Isa Prudence, whose mesmeric education does so much credit to the charming Mlle. Herminie Laurant and her rotund parent.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jacques Lezra|chapter=The Ontology of the Letter in Descartes’s Second Meditation|title=Unspeakable Subjects: The Genealogy of the Event in Early Modern Europe|location=Stanford, Calif.|publisher=w:Stanford University Press|year=1997|page=81|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BKkxRLZ5EI8C&pg=PA81|isbn=978-0-8047-2778-5|passage={{...}} Minerva must never be confused with Santa Maria, for all that they occupy the same discursive space, for all that they must occupy the same position in [[w:Sigmund Freud|[Sigmund] Freud]]'s account of the mind, any more than ''{{w|The Interpretation of Dreams}}'' can be confused with '''oneiromancies''' and dream-books of the past, or Freud's account of the Eternal City with [[w:René Descartes|[René] Descartes]]'s.}}

